@ 17.7.2.2.1 - Old Low Franconian to Middle Dutch
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Middle Dutch”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Middle_Dutch>; and Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Hieronymous Bosch”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Hieronymus_Bosch> Umlaut phonemicizes, but only for umlauts of non-dipthongal short vowels (except in extreme eastern dialects); [ʏ] becomes a phoneme
	u:[+long] > y:[+long]
	iu > ju / #_ (in some northern dialect͡s)
	iu > {y:[+long],io} (outcome varies depending upon dialect; the former seems more typical)
	iw > yw (dialectal)
	{ {ie,ia,io}, uo } > { iə, uə }
	{ f, θ, s } > { v, ð, z } / syllable-initially (h → ɦ?)
	V > ə / if short and unstressed
	f > x, ç / _t (the former seems to have occurred in northern dialect͡s, the latter in southern ones)
	{ θ, ð } > { t, d }
	{u:[+long],uw} u > ɔw o (except in the southeast)
	{ {ol,al}, {ar,er}, or } > { ɔu, a:[+long]r, o:[+long]r } / _C:[+dental]
	V:[-long +stress] > V:[+long] / in open syllables (ʏ → {œ:[+long],ø:[+long]} here but this is not phonemically important; there seem to have been qualitative differences between original long vowels and long vowels resulting from this change—lengthened i:[+long] seems to have become e:[+long], but lengthened a:[+long] merged with original a:[+long]); does not affect original long vowels or vowels in diphthongs